[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64873,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Vaginal cysts are subepithelial cysts lined by squamous, M&uuml;llerian, mesonephric, or transitional epithelium.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:07:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64874,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:07:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64875,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>GA14.Y Other specified acquired abnormalities of vagina</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:07:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64876,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> M&uuml;llerian cyst; mesonephric cyst; Gartner duct cyst; inclusion cyst; urothelial cyst</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:07:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64877,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:07:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64878,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>M&uuml;llerian cysts are most common in the anterior or lateral wall; mesonephric cysts are typically located in the lateral wall.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:07:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64879,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Vaginal cysts are most commonly diagnosed in adults (average patient age: 38&nbsp;years; range: 19&ndash;75&nbsp;years) {3957551; 18390079}. Some patients are asymptomatic and the cyst is an incidental finding, but larger cysts may present with a lump, pain, or urinary symptoms.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:07:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64880,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Inclusion cysts {1143723} and M&uuml;llerian cysts {3957551} are the most common.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:07:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64881,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:07:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64882,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mesonephric (Gartner duct) cysts result from dilatation of mesonephric duct remnants. Some squamous-lined inclusion cysts are seen at sites of prior trauma and are secondary to epithelial entrapment. Urothelial-lined cysts may derive from periurethral glands.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:07:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64883,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Grossly, there is a simple thin-walled cyst, typically containing mucinous or serous fluid.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:07:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64884,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>M&uuml;llerian cysts are lined by mucinous, tubal (ciliated), or endometrioid-type epithelium. Mesonephric cysts are lined by non-mucinous, non-ciliated cuboidal to flattened epithelium. Squamous inclusion cysts are lined by stratified squamous epithelium. Urothelial cysts are lined at least in part by transitional epithelium {1143723; 3957551; 12913681; 2212683; 18390079}. Immunohistochemically, M&uuml;llerian cysts are typically positive for PAX8, ER, and PR; mesonephric cysts are negative for hormone receptors and positive for GATA3.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-29T12:34:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64885,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:07:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
